  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "They didn't lead once. That was the game.",
    "what_this_means": "Ray gave them five innings and four earned runs. A performance they could have survived if the bats had matched it. Instead, they scraped two runs across eight at-bats with runners in scoring position, left runners on in four separate innings, and never mounted anything that resembled pressure. The seventh inning was the breaking point: two runners on, two outs, three straight empty at-bats with a chance to tie. Tatis hit one early for show, Merrill drove one in, and then nothing. Houston's pitching was good. The Padres' offense made it look great. Matsui and Peralta held the damage to one run each across four innings. Competent bridge work into a game that was already decided. But there was nothing for them to protect. This is a team that keeps leaving runners on in situations where contact matters most. The margin between fragile and finished is getting thin.",
    "what_to_watch": "Peter Lambert takes the ball tonight for Houston with a 3.06 ERA. He's found his footing as a starter, and the Padres' inability to string together hits in the middle innings suggests that's exactly the kind of pitcher that will live another day against this lineup.",
